  "content": {
    "id": "p56-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "",
    "prompt": "Show these expressions are rational by rationalising the denominator.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 3,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p56-q8-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{1}{3+\\sqrt6}+\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt6}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1$, which is rational; the $\\sqrt6$ terms cancel.",
          "worked": "Multiply the first fraction by $\\dfrac{3-\\sqrt6}{3-\\sqrt6}$ and the second by $\\dfrac{\\sqrt6}{\\sqrt6}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{3+\\sqrt6}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt6}&=\\frac{3-\\sqrt6}{3^2-(\\sqrt6)^2}+\\frac{2\\sqrt6}{6}\\\\&=\\frac{3-\\sqrt6}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt6}{3}\\\\&=\\frac{3}{3}\\\\&=1\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore the expression is rational.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p56-q8-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{4}{2+\\sqrt2}+\\dfrac{1}{3-2\\sqrt2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$7$, which is rational; the $\\sqrt2$ terms cancel.",
          "worked": "Multiply the fractions by $\\dfrac{2-\\sqrt2}{2-\\sqrt2}$ and $\\dfrac{3+2\\sqrt2}{3+2\\sqrt2}$ respectively.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{4}{2+\\sqrt2}+\\frac{1}{3-2\\sqrt2}&=\\frac{4(2-\\sqrt2)}{4-2}+\\frac{3+2\\sqrt2}{9-8}\\\\&=4-2\\sqrt2+3+2\\sqrt2\\\\&=7\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore the expression is rational.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p56-q8-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{8}{3-\\sqrt7}-\\dfrac{6}{2\\sqrt7-5}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2$, which is rational; the $\\sqrt7$ terms cancel.",
          "worked": "Multiply the fractions by $\\dfrac{3+\\sqrt7}{3+\\sqrt7}$ and $\\dfrac{2\\sqrt7+5}{2\\sqrt7+5}$ respectively.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{8}{3-\\sqrt7}-\\frac{6}{2\\sqrt7-5}&=\\frac{8(3+\\sqrt7)}{9-7}-\\frac{6(2\\sqrt7+5)}{28-25}\\\\&=12+4\\sqrt7-(4\\sqrt7+10)\\\\&=2\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore the expression is rational.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      }
    ]
  },
